# Push to Origin, GitHub, and Azure

A push to Origin is incomplete until the same branch is on GitHub and that commit is deployed to Azure. Do this every time you push to the `origin` remote. Origin stays the remote used for pull requests.

1. Push to Origin: `git push -u origin HEAD`.
2. Push that same branch to GitHub. The remote name is `github` and the URL is `https://github.com/rloria/Academia_Georgetown.git`. Add the remote when it is missing, then `git push -u github HEAD`.
3. Deploy that same commit to the Static Web App `academiageorgetown` in resource group `AcademiaGeorgetown` (West Europe). From the repository root, with Node 22:

```bash
npm ci && npm run build
rm -rf dist/api
TOKEN=$(az staticwebapp secrets list --name academiageorgetown --resource-group AcademiaGeorgetown --query properties.apiKey -o tsv)
npx @azure/static-web-apps-cli deploy ./dist \
  --api-location ./api \
  --api-language node \
  --api-version 22 \
  --deployment-token "$TOKEN" \
  --env production
```

Use `--env production` only for `master` and `main`. For every other branch, deploy a preview environment. Its name must be 1–16 characters, lowercase letters, numbers, and hyphens, and must not start or end with a hyphen. Derive it from the branch by lowercasing, replacing `/` with `-`, and shortening to 16 characters. Do not print the deployment token. Do not force-push. If the GitHub push or the Azure deploy fails, say so and retry; do not treat the Origin push as the finished release.
